1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,363
Screenshots pile up as Screenshot, date, time, and nobody can find the one they need.

2
00:00:06,363 --> 00:00:08,384
Bowerbird is the file mate.

3
00:00:08,384 --> 00:00:13,923
It reads them, proposes honest names, and moves nothing until you approve.

4
00:00:14,173 --> 00:00:15,547
Bowerbird runs on one PC.

5
00:00:15,547 --> 00:00:21,123
A PowerShell launcher starts the engine, and your browser talks to it on localhost.

6
00:00:21,823 --> 00:00:28,122
The engine indexes and searches, proposes renames, and organizes by rules.

7
00:00:28,122 --> 00:00:30,974
The index is encrypted on disk.

8
00:00:31,674 --> 00:00:37,426
Model calls go through Fairlead, with Bowerbird's own key from Credential Manager.

9
00:00:37,426 --> 00:00:39,279
The OpenAI key stays in Lazarette.

10
00:00:39,279 --> 00:00:44,673
Semaphore export and SnapShackle names exist in the code, but aren't shown here.

11
00:00:45,373 --> 00:00:47,670
One rule holds it together.

12
00:00:47,670 --> 00:00:52,333
Nothing is renamed until you type APPLY, and a journal can undo it.

13
00:00:53,030 --> 00:00:57,569
The OpenAI key is already in Lazarette, shared to Fairlead by name.

14
00:00:57,569 --> 00:01:00,253
Fairlead sees only what you share.

15
00:01:00,953 --> 00:01:02,648
Fairlead gives Bowerbird its own key.

16
00:01:02,648 --> 00:01:07,662
Pick Bowerbird, install, and it lands in Windows Credential Manager.

17
00:01:08,292 --> 00:01:12,955
Seven invented screenshots, every one named Screenshot, date, time.

18
00:01:13,655 --> 00:01:14,683
In Smart Rename, tick AI vision.

19
00:01:15,425 --> 00:01:19,074
Bowerbird asks before any image leaves this PC.

20
00:01:19,074 --> 00:01:21,645
These are synthetic, so send them.

21
00:01:21,645 --> 00:01:28,936
Seven live vision calls go through Fairlead to the cloud route, because Fairlead has no local vision model here.

22
00:01:29,636 --> 00:01:33,997
Seven proposals, each with its reason and a confidence.

23
00:01:33,997 --> 00:01:35,956
Nothing has moved yet.

24
00:01:36,656 --> 00:01:38,230
To apply, type APPLY.

25
00:01:38,230 --> 00:01:41,169
The files are renamed exactly as previewed.

26
00:01:41,869 --> 00:01:43,649
Explorer shows the new names.

27
00:01:44,296 --> 00:01:45,281
Nothing was deleted.

28
00:01:45,981 --> 00:01:47,091
Undo has its own gate.

29
00:01:47,720 --> 00:01:49,921
Type UNDO, and every original name comes back.

30
00:01:50,171 --> 00:01:51,354
Bowerbird proposes.

31
00:01:51,354 --> 00:01:52,926
People decide.

32
00:01:52,926 --> 00:02:00,131
The names are better, the moves are reversible, and the model only ever saw what you let it see.

33
00:02:00,131 --> 00:02:01,890
That's the watch.
